# frozen_string_literal: true

=begin
  uc-probe: in-game reachability probe for the uber-combat hunting-zone table.

  Walks a live character at hunting zones in expanding order, nearest first,
  and records which ones it cannot reach. The point is the premium residue:
  190 of the 363 zones in base-uc-zones.yaml still carry premium: null, the
  gate falls OPEN on an unknown (uc_zone_picker.rb#premium_locked?), and no
  offline source can close them. Elanthipedia's {{Critter}} infobox describes
  a CREATURE, and premium access gates ROOMS, so a wiki "not premium" rules
  out the creature and never the rooms. Walking there is the only evidence
  that settles it.

  Spec: notes/uber-combat/37-zone-probe-spec.md, INCLUDING its 2026-09-03
  amendments A1 to A5 at the top of that file. Runtime facts this script
  depends on are cited in notes/uber-combat/39-probe-runtime-facts.md.

  ALL of the decision logic lives in lib/uc_probe.rb and is pure and tested.
  This file is the world adapter and the presentation, nothing more -- the
  same split uc-leg.lic keeps against LegOverlay/LegWriter.

    ;uc-probe             -- PLAN ONLY. Read-only. Walks nowhere. Prints the
                             partition census and the order it would take.
    ;uc-probe run <n>     -- probe until <n> travel attempts are spent.
    ;uc-probe report      -- print everything recorded so far and write the
                             results file the merge tool reads.

  THE DEFAULT MODE WALKS NOWHERE ON PURPOSE. Every other script in this
  project shipped read-only first, and that discipline caught two real bugs
  before anything could act on them. A run is also long: 331 zones at real
  travel speed is hours of unattended walking into hostile territory, so the
  budget argument to `run` is REQUIRED and there is deliberately no way to
  ask for the whole table in one command.

  WHY THE BUDGET IS COUNTED IN TRAVEL ATTEMPTS, not zones: a reached zone
  costs one attempt and a blocked one costs up to MAX_ROOMS_PER_ZONE, so
  attempts are what actually consume the wall clock. Counting zones would
  let `run 10` mean anything from 10 to 30 walks.

  Results accumulate in CharSettings across runs and are written out as YAML
  for notes/uber-combat/tools/merge-probe-results.rb to merge. THIS SCRIPT
  NEVER WRITES base-uc-zones.yaml, and never writes premium: anywhere. A
  probe result is reachability evidence, not a premium verdict -- section 6
  of the spec lists twelve reasons a zone can be unreachable and the probe
  can tell almost none of them apart.

  Examples:
    ;uc-probe
    ;uc-probe run 10
    ;uc-probe report
=end

# load (not require): edits to the uber-combat lib take effect without
# restarting Lich. Same rationale as uc-zones.lic's load of its own libs.
lib_dir = File.join(SCRIPT_DIR, 'custom', 'lib')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_character.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_zone_table.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_probe.rb')

require 'yaml'
require 'time'

# The world contract UberCombat::Probe::Session is built against (see that
# class's header comment). Every Map, Room, DRCT, Flags and DRStats call in
# this script lives in here, so the decision core stays pure and the whole
# loop is driven by a plain double in spec/uc_probe_spec.rb.
class ProbeWorld
  # Our own Flags key. It must not collide with the two DRCT.walk_to
  # registers and DELETES in its own ensure block -- 'travel-closed-shop'
  # and 'travel-engaged' (common-travel.rb:223-224, 259-261). A collision
  # would have walk_to quietly remove our matcher mid-run and every later
  # zone would report blocked_silent.
  BLOCKED_FLAG = 'uc-probe-blocked'

  # Read back after each attempt rather than registered by us: walk_to owns
  # this key's lifetime, so we only ever LOOK at it, between the add and the
  # delete. An engaged attempt is weaker evidence and the record says so.
  ENGAGED_FLAG = 'travel-engaged'

  # The last five are lifted verbatim from Lich's own move() failure regexes
  # (global_defs.rb:628 and :633), so the probe recognises everything move
  # recognises. 'bars your way' is added because the transcript that started
  # this work proved move does NOT recognise it (spec section 0.4) -- the
  # character was refused, move saw nothing, and go2 reported a successful
  # travel time.
  #
  # Flags.add compiles a String into a case-insensitive REGEXP, not a
  # literal substring (events.rb:19). Keep these free of regex metacharacters
  # or escape them.
  # This list is OPEN, not closed. Every refusal a run discovers belongs
  # here, and until one is added it produces blocked_silent, which is the
  # safe direction. Two have been added from real runs so far, both found by
  # watching go2 grind against them:
  #   'bars your way'   -- Baearholt's farmhand
  #   'air shimmers'    -- the barrier into Barana's Shipyard lumber stacks,
  #                        which cost three full stall timeouts before it was
  #                        recognised. Lich's move() does not know this one
  #                        either, so each try burned the whole 10 second
  #                        giveup window ("no recognized response").
  BLOCKING_LINES = [
    'bars your way',
    'The air shimmers in front of you',
    'invisible force is holding you back',
    'You may not pass',
    "Sorry, you aren't allowed to enter here",
    'prevents you from entering',
    'Please leave promptly',
    'become impassable'
  ].freeze

  # Conservative because THE PROBE NEVER FIGHTS. It walks in, looks at the
  # room, and leaves. Any health loss at all therefore means something
  # attacked the character while it stood there, which is reason enough to
  # stop an unattended run. These are starting values and they are the first
  # thing to tune after a real run.
  HEALTH_FLOOR = 75
  SPIRIT_FLOOR = 50

  # How often the deadline watchdog wakes, and how long it waits for the
  # blocked call to notice. See #walk_to for why a thread is needed at all.
  # Once a second, not every two: the restart counter below reads an edge on
  # a global that can be true for as little as go2's own half-second pause on
  # its first restart (go2.lic:2152-2157). A cheaper tick misses more edges.
  WATCHDOG_TICK = 1
  WATCHDOG_JOIN = 5

  # THE EARLY KILL, and it is the difference between a usable probe and an
  # unusable one. On the first live run undead_gerbils cost about 190 seconds
  # of go2 restarting into the same farmhand -- 90 s per room, twice, ended
  # each time by walk_to's own stall watchdog (common-travel.rb:245-252)
  # rather than by anything the probe did:
  #
  #   [go2]>climb stile
  #   A worried-looking farmhand bars your way, ...
  #   [go2]>north     You can't go there.    [go2: move: failed]
  #   [go2]>south     You can't go there.    [go2: move: failed]
  #   [go2: restarting script...]            <- and again, and again
  #
  # The moment that line is captured, THE PROBE ALREADY HAS WHAT IT CAME FOR.
  # A blocked verdict needs the guard's words and nothing else, so every
  # further restart adds spam and risk and no evidence at all.
  #
  # The grace exists because a captured line is not always final: go2 may yet
  # route around the refusal by another path. Roughly three of go2's restart
  # cycles is enough to see whether it is making progress. Measured against
  # that run, a room ends about 22 s after the guard first speaks instead of
  # 90, so the zone costs roughly 50 s rather than 190.
  #
  # This kill deliberately does NOT set timed_out. The verdict must be
  # `blocked`, which is real evidence, and never `timeout`, which is the
  # probe admitting it stopped watching.
  BLOCKED_GRACE = 20

  # THE GENERAL CASE, because BLOCKING_LINES will never be complete. Matching
  # known refusal strings is whack-a-mole: the shipyard barrier was not in the
  # list, so nothing fired and the probe fell through to walk_to's 90 second
  # stall timer, three times over, while a ship's rat walked up.
  #
  # AN EARLIER VERSION COUNTED TIME WITHOUT A ROOM CHANGE, AND THAT WAS WRONG
  # (user). Standing still is not the same as being stuck. A ferry, a paid
  # passage, an escort leg -- all of them hold a character in one room while
  # travel is proceeding exactly as it should, and a 45 second stall rule cut
  # those short.
  #
  # COUNT go2's OWN RESTARTS INSTEAD. go2 restarts only when a movement step
  # FAILED, so the count rises for refusals and stays flat through every
  # legitimate wait, which is precisely the distinction the stall clock could
  # not draw. `$go2_restart` is a process-wide global (go2.lic:2143), set at
  # each failure and cleared at :2305, and go2 sleeps 5 s inside that window
  # on every restart after the first (:2159-2163), so a once-a-second poll
  # sees the edge. The very first restart has no sleep and can be missed;
  # that only shifts the effective threshold by one, which is why this is set
  # generously rather than tight.
  #
  # Six is deliberately loose. A ferry that fails a move or two while the
  # gangplank is still up must not be cut off. It still bounds the shipyard
  # case at about a minute instead of 270 seconds.
  #
  # Like the blocked kill, this does NOT set timed_out. The verdict becomes
  # blocked_silent, which is the honest answer: something stopped us and the
  # probe did not recognise what. Those records are the queue of lines to add
  # to BLOCKING_LINES.
  MAX_GO2_RESTARTS = 6

  attr_reader :attempts, :abort_detail

  def initialize(budget)
    @budget = budget
    @attempts = 0
    @abort_detail = nil
    # Map.rooms_by_tag returns a fresh copy on every call (map_base.rb:550)
    # and Probe.rank asks for every probeable zone on every iteration. Over
    # a 331-zone run that is tens of thousands of array copies for data that
    # cannot change while the script runs.
    @rooms_cache = {}
    Flags.add(BLOCKED_FLAG, *BLOCKING_LINES)
  end

  def cleanup
    Flags.delete(BLOCKED_FLAG)
  end

  def rooms_for_tag(key)
    @rooms_cache[key] ||= Map.rooms_by_tag(key)
  end

  def current_room_id
    Room.current&.id
  end

  # Room#dijkstra returns [previous, shortest_distances], both Hashes keyed
  # by room id, weighted by timeto SECONDS rather than hop count
  # (map_base.rb:774, :814-820). One call prices every candidate room at
  # once, which is why Session calls this once per iteration and never once
  # per zone.
  def distances_from(room_id)
    room = Map[room_id]
    return nil unless room

    _previous, distances = room.dijkstra
    distances
  end

  def reset_capture
    Flags.reset(BLOCKED_FLAG)
  end

  # Flags[key] holds the MatchData, not true (drparser.rb:93). MatchData#string
  # is the WHOLE line; [0] would be just the fragment our pattern matched,
  # which throws away the guard's actual words -- and those words are the
  # entire value of a blocked record, because a human adjudicates from them.
  def blocking_line
    match = Flags[BLOCKED_FLAG]
    return nil unless match

    match.respond_to?(:string) ? match.string.strip : nil
  end

  def engaged?
    Flags[ENGAGED_FLAG] ? true : false
  end

  def now
    Time.now
  end

  # Printed before every walk. An attempt can run the best part of a minute
  # and ends with go2 being killed, so without this the operator watches a
  # silent pause and cannot tell "moving on to a new zone" from "wedged on
  # the same one again". The room counter matters for the same reason: it
  # says whether this is a second entrance to the zone just refused, or a
  # fresh zone entirely.
  #
  # The band rides along because it is the one zone fact that still varies
  # here and it lets the band filter be checked by eye. The premium flag
  # used to be printed too and is not any more: every zone a run reaches is
  # one with premium unset, so the column said `nil` on every line.
  def announce(zone, room_id, index, count)
    DRC.message(format('-- [%d/%d] %s  room %d (%d of %d)  band %s-%s',
                       @attempts + 1, @budget, zone.key, room_id, index, count,
                       zone.rank_min, zone.rank_max))
  end

  # Both names, because dr-scripts has two: `escort` and `bescort`
  # (dr-scripts/bescort.lic). walk_to tests exactly this pair before deciding
  # its own stall timer means anything (common-travel.rb:253-255).
  def escorting?
    Script.running?('escort') || Script.running?('bescort')
  end

  # DRCT.walk_to BLOCKS for the whole trip: its polling loop
  # (common-travel.rb:227-259) runs on this thread. So the caller cannot
  # time itself out, and the spec's original "wrap each walk_to in a
  # deadline" is not implementable as written (amendment A1). A watchdog
  # THREAD is the way, and it has to kill go2 REPEATEDLY:
  #
  #   walk_to's engaged branch (common-travel.rb:238-244) relaunches go2
  #   unconditionally -- it ignores restart_on_fail -- and resets its own
  #   timer, so walk_to's 90 second stall watchdog can never fire while the
  #   character is being attacked over and over. A hunting zone is exactly
  #   where that state persists. One kill would simply be undone.
  #
  # Killing go2 does end walk_to's loop, because Script.running.include? is
  # its only condition. kill_script is async by default (script.rb:1776-1784),
  # so the kill returns before go2's own before_dying revert of the map edges
  # it nulled has finished -- that is why the watchdog keeps ticking rather
  # than killing once and exiting.
  #
  # walk_to(room, false) suppresses only walk_to's OWN outer recursion. go2
  # still restarts internally, which is the right level: we want one LOGICAL
  # attempt, not one go2 process.
  def walk_to(room_id, deadline)
    @attempts += 1
    started = Time.now
    done = false
    timed_out = false

    watchdog = Thread.new do
      clock = Time.now
      blocked_at = nil
      restarts = 0
      was_restarting = false

      until done
        if escorting?
          # SOMEONE ELSE IS DRIVING. An escort moves the character on its own
          # schedule and go2 is not in charge, so none of these signals mean
          # what they usually mean. walk_to holds off for the same reason
          # (common-travel.rb:253-255). The deadline clock is pushed forward
          # rather than merely ignored, so an escort leg cannot consume the
          # budget the walk itself still needs afterwards.
          clock = Time.now
          blocked_at = nil
          restarts = 0
          sleep WATCHDOG_TICK
          next
        end

        # Count the false-to-true edges of go2's own restart flag. See
        # MAX_GO2_RESTARTS for why this and not a clock.
        restarting = $go2_restart ? true : false
        restarts += 1 if restarting && !was_restarting
        was_restarting = restarting

        blocked_at ||= Time.now if Flags[BLOCKED_FLAG]

        if Time.now - clock > deadline
          timed_out = true
          kill_script('go2')
        elsif blocked_at && Time.now - blocked_at > BLOCKED_GRACE
          # Evidence in hand. Kill WITHOUT setting timed_out, so the verdict
          # stays `blocked`. See BLOCKED_GRACE above.
          kill_script('go2')
        elsif restarts >= MAX_GO2_RESTARTS
          # go2 is failing moves, not waiting. Also no timed_out, so this
          # reads as blocked_silent. See MAX_GO2_RESTARTS above.
          kill_script('go2')
        end
        sleep WATCHDOG_TICK
      end
    end

    arrived = begin
      DRCT.walk_to(room_id, false)
    rescue StandardError => e
      # walk_to drops its Room.current&.id guard at common-travel.rb:265 and
      # :272 (amendment A2), so a room that becomes unresolvable mid-trip can
      # raise here. A raised attempt is a failed attempt, never a crashed run.
      DRC.message(format('uc-probe: walk_to(%d) raised %s: %s', room_id, e.class, e.message))
      false
    ensure
      done = true
    end

    watchdog.join(WATCHDOG_JOIN)
    watchdog.kill if watchdog.alive?

    [arrived, timed_out, (Time.now - started).round(1)]
  end

  # Checked by Session between every zone AND between the rooms of one zone.
  # Every reader here is free -- a cached XML or class-variable read, no game
  # command (39-probe-runtime-facts.md Q6). DRCH.check_health is deliberately
  # NOT in this ladder: it issues a live `health` command, and a ladder that
  # costs a command cannot be polled this often.
  #
  # Encumbrance is deliberately absent too. The probe never loots and never
  # fights, so encumbrance cannot change during a run, and inventing a
  # threshold for a value that cannot move would be noise.
  def abort_reason
    return :budget_spent if @attempts >= @budget
    return note(:dead) if dead?
    return note(:health, DRStats.health) if DRStats.health < HEALTH_FLOOR
    return note(:bleeding) if checkbleeding
    return note(:spirit, DRStats.spirit) if DRStats.spirit < SPIRIT_FLOOR

    nil
  end

  private

  def note(reason, value = nil)
    @abort_detail = value
    reason
  end
end

# Owns the modes, the run metadata, and persistence. No decision logic --
# every verdict, every ordering choice and the whole loop belong to
# UberCombat::Probe (lib/uc_probe.rb).
class UcProbe
  # No 'base-' prefix, and that is mandatory rather than stylistic: Lich's
  # get_data globs base*.yaml across scripts/data and scripts/data/custom on
  # EVERY call (setup_files.rb:181-190), so a base-prefixed results file here
  # would be silently loaded as profile data by unrelated scripts.
  RESULTS_FILE = 'uc-probe-results.yaml'

  # CharSettings is scoped to Script.current.name (settings.rb:72, :153), so
  # this key lives in uc-probe's own namespace and cannot collide with any
  # other script's. Writes are immediate and Marshal-backed, so a nested Hash
  # round-trips exactly (39-probe-runtime-facts.md Q3).
  RECORDS_KEY = 'probe_records'
  RUN_KEY = 'probe_last_run'

  # Bounded so a run cannot end by hanging in the recovery path itself.
  GOSAFE_TIMEOUT = 300

  def initialize
    @zone_table = UberCombat::ZoneTable.from_game_data
    @tier = Lich::Common::Account.subscription
    @character = UberCombat::Character.new
    # The CONCENTRATED pole, which is simply the highest defence
    # (uc_character.rb:98). It is the more permissive of the two poles, and
    # the picker escalates to it when spread will not admit a zone, so using
    # it here keeps the probe from deferring a zone the picker would still
    # be willing to select. Read live on every invocation, never cached
    # across runs: the character advances, and a stale metric would defer
    # zones they have already grown into.
    @defence = @character.defensive_metric(:concentrated)
  end

  # PLAN ONLY. Issues no game command, walks nowhere, writes nothing.
  def plan
    banner
    world = ProbeWorld.new(0)
    begin
      partition = UberCombat::Probe.partition(@zone_table.zones, world.method(:rooms_for_tag),
                                              defence: @defence)
      report_partition(partition)
      report_order(world, partition.probeable)
    ensure
      world.cleanup
    end
    report_progress
  end

  def run(budget)
    return unless tier_known?

    banner
    world = ProbeWorld.new(budget)
    unless preflight(world)
      world.cleanup
      return
    end

    DRC.message(format('-- Probing, budget %d travel attempt(s) --', budget))
    warn_about_stop_for_dead
    started = Time.now.utc.iso8601

    outcome = nil
    begin
      session = UberCombat::Probe::Session.new(pending_zones, world, tier: @tier, meta: meta,
                                               defence: @defence)
      outcome = session.run
    ensure
      world.cleanup
    end

    # Merged, never replaced. Records accumulate across runs and across
    # characters, and Probe.record's reachability hash is keyed by account
    # tier precisely so a premium result and a basic result on the same zone
    # can both survive (lib/uc_probe.rb, .record's comment).
    store(outcome.records)
    store_run(started, outcome.aborted)
    report_outcome(world, outcome)
    recover(world, outcome.aborted)
    write_results
  end

  def report
    banner
    report_progress
    write_results
  end

  private

  # A run whose account tier is unknown is worthless -- every verdict it
  # produces is unattributable, and the whole design turns on comparing a
  # premium result against a basic one. Account.subscription is nil until a
  # character logs in through Lich's own auth path (account.rb:5, :26).
  def tier_known?
    return true if @tier

    DRC.message('uc-probe: Account.subscription is nil, so the account tier is unknown.')
    DRC.message('  Every result would be unattributable. Refusing to probe.')
    false
  end

  # The abort ladder is checked before the first zone anyway, so without this
  # a character who is already hurt would "start" a run, settle nothing, and
  # then be walked to the saferoom by the recovery path -- which looks like a
  # malfunction rather than a refusal. Saying so up front is the difference
  # between a script that declined and a script that misbehaved.
  #
  # The budget is excluded deliberately: a fresh world reports :budget_spent
  # only when the caller asked for zero attempts, which the argument parser
  # already rejects.
  def preflight(world)
    reason = world.abort_reason
    return true if reason.nil? || reason == :budget_spent

    detail = world.abort_detail ? " (#{world.abort_detail})" : ''
    DRC.message(format('uc-probe: NOT starting -- %s%s.', reason, detail))
    DRC.message('  The probe never fights, so it will not walk a character that is already in trouble.')
    false
  end

  # go2 PAUSES ITSELF when it sees a corpse (go2.lic:2399, :2465), which in an
  # unattended run parks the character next to a dead player until the
  # deadline kills it -- one wasted deadline per attempt, and a verdict of
  # :timeout that means nothing. The setting is go2's, and CharSettings is
  # scoped to the CALLING script, so reading it needs the scope named
  # explicitly (39-probe-runtime-facts.md Q8).
  def warn_about_stop_for_dead
    stop_for_dead = Lich::Common::Settings.get_scoped_setting(
      CharSettings.active_scope, 'stop for dead', script_name: 'go2'
    )
    return unless stop_for_dead

    DRC.message("uc-probe: WARNING -- go2's 'stop for dead' is on.")
    DRC.message('  go2 pauses itself next to a corpse, which an unattended run reads as a timeout.')
  rescue StandardError => e
    DRC.message("uc-probe: could not read go2's 'stop for dead' setting (#{e.class}). Continuing.")
  end

  # There is no runtime accessor for the loaded map file, so this is INFERRED
  # rather than confirmed (39-probe-runtime-facts.md Q9). It is sound: Lich
  # tries json_map_files in order and returns on the first that parses
  # (map_base.rb:337-357), and that list is sorted newest-first
  # (map_base.rb:428-437). Recorded because a verdict is only comparable
  # against results from the same map data.
  def map_name
    File.basename(Map.json_map_files.first.to_s)
  rescue StandardError
    'unknown'
  end

  def meta
    { character: XMLData.name, game: XMLData.game, map: map_name }
  end

  def stored
    CharSettings[RECORDS_KEY] || {}
  end

  # A run covers only what no run has settled yet. Session's own "never probe
  # the same zone twice" rule (lib/uc_probe.rb) holds WITHIN one run; this is
  # what makes the rule hold ACROSS runs, and it is what lets a 331-zone job
  # be done in bounded pieces without repeating work or spending the budget
  # on answers already in hand.
  #
  # It settles a timeout too. Retrying one is a human decision, made by
  # clearing that zone out of CharSettings, not something a later run does on
  # its own -- an unattended retry loop on a zone that wedges once would
  # spend every future budget on the same wedge.
  def pending_zones
    settled = stored
    @zone_table.zones.reject { |zone| settled.key?(zone.key) }
  end

  def store(records)
    CharSettings[RECORDS_KEY] = stored.merge(records)
  end

  # The LAST run's timing and stop reason, kept beside the records so that
  # ;uc-probe report can rebuild a complete results file without a run
  # having just happened. The merge tool prints these as the provenance of
  # the file it is reading, and a file that cannot say when it was collected
  # or why it stopped is a file nobody can weigh.
  def store_run(started, aborted)
    CharSettings[RUN_KEY] = {
      'started'  => started,
      'finished' => Time.now.utc.iso8601,
      'aborted'  => aborted&.to_s
    }
  end

  def stored_run
    CharSettings[RUN_KEY] || {}
  end

  def banner
    DRC.message(format('=== uc-probe: %s, tier=%s, defences=%.1f, %d zones loaded ===',
                       XMLData.name, @tier || 'UNKNOWN', @defence, @zone_table.zones.size))
  end

  def report_partition(partition)
    DRC.message('-- Zone partition --')
    DRC.message(format('  probeable      %4d', partition.probeable.size), false)
    DRC.message(format('  escort         %4d  (reached by escort, never walked)', partition.escort.size), false)
    DRC.message(format('  no map tag     %4d  (nothing to walk to)', partition.no_tag.size), false)
    DRC.message(format('  already known  %4d  (premium answered, nothing left to learn)',
                       partition.answered.size), false)
    DRC.message(format('  out of band    %4d  (deferred: defences %.1f cannot survive there)',
                       partition.out_of_band.size, @defence), false)
    DRC.message('  Neither is recorded, and the two mean opposite things: an answered zone', false)
    DRC.message('  has nothing left to give, an out-of-band one has something we cannot go', false)
    DRC.message('  and get. Only the second is a gap, and it closes as the character grows.', false)
    DRC.message(format('  probeable is therefore exactly the %d zones with premium still unset.',
                       partition.probeable.size), false)
  end

  # The same nearest-first ordering the run itself would follow, printed
  # without moving. Probe.rank settles the whole ranking in one call, so this
  # shows the FIRST zone truthfully; every zone after it depends on where the
  # character ends up, which is the point of re-ranking each iteration and is
  # why this list is a preview rather than a schedule.
  def report_order(world, probeable)
    here = world.current_room_id
    unless here
      DRC.message('uc-probe: current room is unknown; cannot compute distance.')
      return
    end

    distances = world.distances_from(here)
    unless distances
      DRC.message('uc-probe: dijkstra failed from the current room.')
      return
    end

    settled = stored
    pending = probeable.reject { |zone| settled.key?(zone.key) }
    DRC.message(format('-- Nearest unprobed zones from room %d (%d still pending) --', here, pending.size))
    preview(pending, distances, world)
  end

  def preview(pending, distances, world)
    rows = pending.filter_map do |zone|
      nearest = world.rooms_for_tag(zone.key)
                     .filter_map { |id| distances[id] && [id, distances[id]] }
                     .min_by { |(_id, dist)| dist }
      nearest && { key: zone.key, room: nearest[0], distance: nearest[1],
                   rooms: world.rooms_for_tag(zone.key).size,
                   band: format('%s-%s', zone.rank_min, zone.rank_max) }
    end

    if rows.empty?
      DRC.message('  (none reachable)', false)
      return
    end

    # The band is printed so the filter can be checked by eye: every row here
    # must have a floor at or below the defences shown in the banner. ROOMS
    # replaced a premium column that now reads `nil` on every line, and it
    # earns the space -- it is the number of entrances a blocked verdict has
    # to get through, so it predicts what a refusal will cost.
    DRC.message(format('  %8s %8s %-30s %-10s %s', 'DIST', 'ROOM', 'ZONE', 'BAND', 'ROOMS'), false)
    rows.sort_by { |row| row[:distance] }.first(15).each do |row|
      DRC.message(format('  %8.1f %8d %-30s %-10s %d',
                         row[:distance], row[:room], row[:key], row[:band], row[:rooms]), false)
    end
    DRC.message(format('  ... %d more', rows.size - 15), false) if rows.size > 15
  end

  def report_outcome(world, outcome)
    DRC.message('-- Run outcome --')
    DRC.message(format('  zones settled this run: %d', outcome.records.size), false)
    DRC.message(format('  travel attempts spent:  %d', world.attempts), false)
    DRC.message(format('  skipped, already known: %d', outcome.answered), false)
    DRC.message(format('  deferred, out of band:  %d', outcome.deferred), false)
    if outcome.aborted
      detail = world.abort_detail ? " (#{world.abort_detail})" : ''
      DRC.message(format('  STOPPED: %s%s', outcome.aborted, detail), false)
    end
    tally = outcome.records.values.group_by { |rec| rec['reachability'][@tier]['verdict'] }
    tally.sort_by { |verdict, _| verdict }.each do |verdict, list|
      DRC.message(format('  %-16s %4d', verdict, list.size), false)
    end
  end

  # Only a SAFETY abort goes to the saferoom. A spent budget is the ordinary,
  # expected end of a bounded run, and walking the character across the
  # continent every time a 10-attempt run finishes would cost more travel
  # than the run itself.
  def recover(_world, aborted)
    return if aborted.nil? || aborted == :budget_spent

    DRC.message(format('-- Safety abort (%s): running gosafe --', aborted))
    Script.run_child('gosafe', timeout: GOSAFE_TIMEOUT)
    DRC.message('  gosafe finished.', false)
  rescue StandardError => e
    # Never let the recovery path be the thing that kills the run's report.
    # The records are already stored by the time this runs.
    DRC.message(format('  gosafe did not complete (%s: %s). Recover by hand.', e.class, e.message))
  end

  def report_progress
    records = stored
    DRC.message(format('-- Recorded so far: %d zone(s) --', records.size))
    return if records.empty?

    by_verdict = Hash.new(0)
    records.each_value do |record|
      record['reachability'].each_value { |entry| by_verdict[entry['verdict']] += 1 }
    end
    by_verdict.sort_by { |verdict, _| verdict }.each do |verdict, count|
      DRC.message(format('  %-16s %4d', verdict, count), false)
    end

    blocked = records.select do |_key, record|
      record['reachability'].each_value.any? { |entry| entry['verdict'].start_with?('blocked') }
    end
    report_blocked(blocked)
  end

  # The blocking lines are the whole reason a blocked verdict is worth
  # anything. The probe cannot tell a premium gate from a quest gate, a
  # locked door or stale map data (spec section 6), so a person reads these
  # and adjudicates. Printing the verdict without the line would hand nobody
  # anything to judge.
  def report_blocked(blocked)
    return if blocked.empty?

    DRC.message('  -- Blocked zones, with the line that blocked them --', false)
    blocked.each do |key, record|
      record['reachability'].each do |tier, entry|
        DRC.message(format('    %-30s %-8s %s', key, tier, entry['verdict']), false)
        DRC.message(format('      %s', entry['line']), false) if entry['line']
      end
    end
  end

  # CharSettings[...] DOES NOT RETURN A HASH. It returns a
  # Lich::Common::SettingsProxy (settings_proxy.rb:6), which quacks like one
  # for every read this script does -- merge, each_value, select, size all
  # work -- and then betrays it at exactly one point: to_yaml serialises the
  # PROXY, not the data. The first live run wrote a results file whose
  # `records` key was `!ruby/object:Lich::Common::SettingsProxy` with the real
  # table buried under `target:`, which the merge tool cannot read at all.
  #
  # This is the same class of failure as the get_data OpenStruct bug: an
  # object that behaves like the container it wraps until the one moment it
  # does not, failing in a way no test using plain Hashes could catch.
  #
  # The walk is DEEP rather than a single to_h. The proxy wraps values it
  # hands back on read (Settings.wrap_value_if_container), so a nested level
  # can be a proxy too, and one surviving anywhere in the tree poisons the
  # whole document.
  def plain(value)
    case value
    when Array then value.map { |item| plain(item) }
    when Hash  then value.each_with_object({}) { |(key, item), out| out[key] = plain(item) }
    else
      container = unwrap(value)
      container.nil? ? value : plain(container)
    end
  end

  # The shape has to be discovered by TRYING, not by asking. SettingsProxy
  # overrides respond_to? as `super || @target.respond_to?(...)`
  # (settings_proxy.rb:191), and the proxy defines both to_hash and to_ary
  # itself, so it answers true to both no matter which one its target can
  # actually satisfy -- and the wrong one raises (delegate_conversion's
  # strict branch, settings_proxy.rb:57-59).
  #
  # nil means "not a container, leave it alone". Returning the value itself
  # instead would make #plain recurse forever on any scalar.
  def unwrap(value)
    return nil unless value.respond_to?(:to_hash) || value.respond_to?(:to_ary)

    begin
      value.to_hash
    rescue StandardError
      begin
        value.to_ary
      rescue StandardError
        nil
      end
    end
  end

  def results_path
    File.join(SCRIPT_DIR, 'data', 'custom', RESULTS_FILE)
  end

  # Written whole on every call rather than appended to, because CharSettings
  # is the record of truth for a run in progress and this file is only ever a
  # snapshot of it for the merge tool to read. Regenerating it can therefore
  # never disagree with the database.
  def write_results
    records = stored
    if records.empty?
      DRC.message('-- No results to write --')
      return
    end

    file_meta = meta.transform_keys(&:to_s).merge('tier' => @tier).merge(stored_run)
    payload = { 'meta' => file_meta, 'records' => plain(records) }
    File.write(results_path, payload.to_yaml)
    DRC.message('-- Results written --')
    DRC.message("  #{results_path}", false)
    # The path is spelled out rather than shown as a placeholder in angle
    # brackets. DRC.message sends its text down a channel that strips
    # anything looking like a tag, so the first live run printed the command
    # with its argument silently missing.
    DRC.message("  Merge with: ruby notes/uber-combat/tools/merge-probe-results.rb #{results_path}",
                false)
  end
end

# script is bound to Script.current by Lich's own script-launch eval
# (script.rb:176). vars[1], vars[2] are the whitespace-split tokens
# (script.rb:3098-3099). Same positional convention uc-leg.lic uses.
#
# No argument is the read-only plan -- the case that must never change.
mode = script.vars[1]

case mode
when nil
  UcProbe.new.plan
when /\Arun\z/i
  budget = script.vars[2]
  if budget.nil? || budget !~ /\A[1-9][0-9]*\z/
    DRC.message('uc-probe: run needs a positive attempt budget. Usage: ;uc-probe run <n>')
    DRC.message('  There is no unbounded mode. A full table is hours of unattended walking.')
  else
    UcProbe.new.run(budget.to_i)
  end
when /\Areport\z/i
  UcProbe.new.report
else
  DRC.message("uc-probe: unknown argument '#{mode}'. Usage: ;uc-probe | ;uc-probe run <n> | ;uc-probe report")
end
